---
// Pulse privacy policy (app, waitlist and this website).
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/pulse.css';
import { site, mailto } from '../../config/site';
const email = site.contact.email;
const mail = mailto(email, 'Pulse privacy');
---
<Base title="Pulse privacy policy" description="How the Pulse app, the Pulse beta waitlist and this website use your information." image="/og/pulse.png">
  <div class="pulse">
    <article class="container p-page">
      <div class="prose">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pulse</span>
        <h1>Pulse privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="muted">Last updated: 27 November 2026</p>

        <p>
          Pulse ("the app") is a revision app for medical graduates and interns, made by Nifty Games Studios ("we").
          This page explains what information the app, the Pulse beta waitlist and this website use, and why.
          You can reach us at <a href={mail}>{email}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>1. The beta waitlist</h2>
        <p>When you join the waitlist we collect:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>your name and email address;</li>
          <li>your Google account email, used only to add you to Google Play testing so you can install the beta;</li>
          <li>your stage (intern or PG aspirant) and exam year;</li>
          <li>which creator link you came from, if any.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The form is run by Tally, a form service, which stores the answers for us. We use them only to invite you to the
          Pulse beta and to send you beta updates. To invite you, we add your Google account email to the beta testers list
          in Google Play.
        </p>
        <p>
          We keep this information until the beta ends, or until you ask us to delete it, whichever comes first.
          To ask, email <a href={mail}>{email}</a> from the address you signed up with.
        </p>
        <p>If you follow our WhatsApp Channel for updates, you can leave it at any time.</p>

        <h2>2. This website</h2>
        <p>
          This website uses Google Analytics to see which pages are useful, and only after you allow it in the cookie notice.
          If you decline, no analytics cookies are set. You can change your choice at any time with "Cookie settings" at the
          bottom of every page. If you came from a creator's link, that link's short code is kept in your browser tab only
          while you visit and is passed to the waitlist form.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Signing in with Google (required)</h2>
        <p>
          Pulse asks you to sign in with your Google account when you first open it. We use Google Firebase Authentication for
          this. It gives us your Google account email address, your name, the link to your Google profile photo and a user ID.
          We use them to keep your progress safe in your account and to let you use it on another phone. We never see your
          Google password.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Your learning progress (synced automatically)</h2>
        <p>
          Your learning progress is kept on your phone and copied automatically to your account in Google Firebase (Firestore),
          so it is not lost if you change or reset your phone. This copy includes the cards you have learnt, your review history
          and which answers were right or wrong, XP, streak, achievements, journeys, favourites and settings, and a friend code.
          Only you can read it.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Friends and the weekly leaderboard (only if you turn them on)</h2>
        <p>
          Friends is off until you press "Turn on friends" on the Friends screen. Only then do we publish a profile: the display
          name and emoji avatar you chose, your level, total XP, XP this week and per day, streak and up to three achievement
          badges you pin, plus your friend code. This profile is visible only to you and to the people you accept as friends, and
          a friend request shows the sender's display name and avatar to the person they ask. Friends never see your email, your
          Google photo, your cards or your answers. You can turn friends off at any time and the profile, code and friendships are
          deleted.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Shared journeys and reports</h2>
        <p>
          If you invite a friend to a journey, or accept an invite, Pulse stores the journey (its name and the list of topics),
          who is in it, and, for each member, the display name, emoji avatar and the list of topics that member has finished. The
          other members of that journey can see this. They never see your answers, cards or scores. Leaving a journey, being
          removed, or the owner stopping it deletes your progress entry for it. When you delete your account in the app, it stops
          the journeys you shared, leaves the ones you joined (removing your progress entries) and declines open invites.
        </p>
        <p>
          When you press the flag on a card or question to report a mistake, Pulse sends the card or question identifier, the
          reason you chose, the optional note you typed (up to 300 characters), your account ID, the app version and the content
          version. It never sends the card text. Only we can read reports, and we use them to correct the content. The app cannot
          delete a report after it was sent: a report is not removed when you delete your account. To have your reports deleted,
          email <a href={mail}>{email}</a> and we remove them.
        </p>

        <h2>7. Usage and crash data (only if you allow it)</h2>
        <p>
          The app asks whether you allow usage data and crash reports. If you say yes, we use Google Firebase Analytics and
          Firebase Crashlytics to learn how the app is used and to fix crashes. If you say no, none of this is sent. You can
          change your mind at any time in Settings.
        </p>
        <table class="p-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Events such as "session finished", counts, and how long answers take</td><td>See which features work and where learners get stuck</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Crash reports: device model, Android version, app version, what the app was doing</td><td>Find and fix bugs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A random installation ID created by Firebase</td><td>Count how many people use the app</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          We never send the text of cards or questions, or your answers, in these events. We do not use the advertising ID,
          we show no ads and we do not sell your data.
        </p>

        <h2>8. Downloading content and photos</h2>
        <p>
          Study content (cards and questions) is downloaded from Google Firebase (Firestore) while you are signed in. Some
          subjects include photographs, such as clinical photos with their credit lines. They are downloaded from a private image
          library hosted on GitHub. Like any website, GitHub can see your phone's internet address when a photo is requested. No
          account details or progress are sent to it.
        </p>

        <h2>9. Notifications</h2>
        <p>If you allow them, the app schedules reminders on your phone. They are created on the device and do not use a server.</p>

        <h2>10. How long we keep data</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Account, synced progress, friends profile, shared journeys and invites: as long as your account exists. They are deleted when you delete your account (section 11).</li>
          <li>Reports about cards or questions (identifiers, reason, note, your user ID): kept until you ask us to delete them by email; deleting your account in the app does not remove them.</li>
          <li>Analytics and crash data: kept by Firebase for a limited period and then deleted automatically.</li>
          <li>Waitlist answers: kept until the beta ends, or deleted sooner when you ask by email.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="delete-account">11. Delete your account</h2>
        <p>You can delete your Pulse account and its data at any time:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>In the app:</strong> Settings &gt; Delete account. This deletes your synced progress, your friend code, your friends profile and friendships, your shared journeys, invites and journey progress (it stops sharing, leaves journeys and declines invites first), and your sign-in account, and clears Pulse's data on that phone. It cannot delete reports you sent about cards or questions: they hold identifiers, a reason, an optional note and your user ID, and we remove them if you ask by email.</li>
          <li><strong>Without the app:</strong> email <a href={mail}>{email}</a> from the Google address you signed in with, with the subject "Delete my Pulse account". We delete the same data, including your reports, within 30 days and reply when it is done.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Analytics and crash data are not linked to your name or email; they expire automatically.</p>

        <h2>12. Who it is shared with</h2>
        <p>
          Only the services that process data for us: Google (Firebase Authentication, Firestore, Analytics and Crashlytics, Google
          Play testing and, if you allow it, Google Analytics on this website), Tally for the waitlist form, and the friends you
          accept (section 5). We do not sell data and we do not share it with advertisers or data brokers. Data sent from the app
          is encrypted in transit.
        </p>

        <h2>13. Age</h2>
        <p>Pulse is meant for adults (18 and over), such as medical graduates and doctors. It is not directed at children.</p>

        <h2>14. Medical disclaimer</h2>
        <p>
          Pulse is for exam revision only. It is not medical advice and must not be used to diagnose or treat patients.
          Always check your standard textbooks.
        </p>

        <h2>15. Your rights and contact</h2>
        <p>
          You can ask us to show, correct or delete the data we hold about you at any time. Write to <a href={mail}>{email}</a>.
          Grievance contact for India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act: Grievance Officer, Nifty Games Studios,
          <a href={mail}>{email}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>16. Changes</h2>
        <p>If we change this policy we will update the date above and, for important changes, tell you in the app.</p>

        <p style="margin-top:2rem"><a href="/pulse/">Back to Pulse</a></p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
